{
  "rules": [
    "When sentiment is consistently zero (0.00), the model exhibits a severe upward bias and fails to capture high-frequency volatility; forecasts should be corrected downward by approximately 15-20% of the predicted range.",
    "The model systematically underestimates immediate post-spike mean reversion, assuming linear continuation after sudden price jumps while true outcomes show rapid reversal within 3-5 days.",
    "In low-sentiment environments (sentiment < 0.5), the model's forecast horizon is too long and smooth; apply a volatility correction factor that increases the error margin by 2x for the first 10 days of the forecast period.",
    "When sentiment spikes to 1.0, the model often ignores the momentum entirely, resulting in forecasts that are significantly lower than actual breakout highs; add a positive bias equal to the last 3 days' price increase.",
    "The model consistently predicts a steady upward drift even when historical data shows a clear downward trend or consolidation; reverse the forecast direction if the last 5 days of history show a net decline."
  ]
}
